---
id: form-composition
title: Form Composition
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A common criticism of TanStack Form is its verbosity out-of-the-box. While this "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"can"}]},{"type":"text","value":" be useful for educational purposes - helping enforce understanding our APIs - it's not ideal in production use cases."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"As a result, while "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.Field"},{"type":"text","value":" enables the most powerful and flexible usage of TanStack Form, we provide APIs that wrap it and make your application code less verbose."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-form-methods","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Form Methods"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The most powerful way to compose forms is to create custom form methods. This allows you to create a form method that is tailored to your application's needs, including pre-bound custom UI components and more."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"At its most basic, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFormCreator"},{"type":"text","value":" is a function that returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createAppForm"},{"type":"text","value":" method."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This un-customized "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createAppForm"},{"type":"text","value":" method is identical to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createForm"},{"type":"text","value":", but that will quickly change as we add more options to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFormCreator"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"// form-context.ts\nimport { createFormCreatorContexts } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\n// export useFieldContext and useFormContext for use in your custom components\nexport const { useFieldContext, useFormContext } = createFormCreatorContexts()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"// form.ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\nexport const { createAppForm } = createFormCreator({\n  // We'll learn more about these options later\n  fieldComponents: {},\n  formComponents: {},\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- App.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    // Supports all createForm options\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<form.Field name=\"firstName\">\n  <!-- ... -->\n</form.Field>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"pre-bound-field-components","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pre-bound Field Components"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once this scaffolding is in place, you can start adding custom field and form components to your form method."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note: the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useFieldContext"},{"type":"text","value":" must be the same one exported from your custom form context"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- text-field.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { useFieldContext } from './form-context.js'\n\n  // The `Field` infers that it should have a `value` type of `string`\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n\n  const { label }: { label: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<label>\n  <div>{label}</div>\n  <input\n    value={field.state.value}\n    oninput={(e) => field.handleChange((e.target as HTMLInputElement).value)}\n  />\n</label>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You're then able to register this component with your form method."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\nimport TextField from './text-field.svelte'\n\nexport const { createAppForm } = createFormCreator({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {},\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"And use it in your form:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<!-- Notice the `AppField` instead of `Field`; `AppField` provides the required context -->\n<form.AppField name=\"firstName\">\n  {#snippet children(field)}\n    <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />\n  {/snippet}\n</form.AppField>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This not only allows you to reuse the UI of your shared component, but retains the type-safety you'd expect from TanStack Form: Typo "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":" and get a TypeScript error."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"pre-bound-form-components","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pre-bound Form Components"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.AppField"},{"type":"text","value":" solves many of the problems with Field boilerplate and reusability, it doesn't solve the problem of "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"form"}]},{"type":"text","value":" boilerplate and reusability."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In particular, being able to share instances of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" for, say, a reactive form submission button is a common usecase."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- subscribe-button.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { useFormContext } from './form-context.js'\n\n  const form = useFormContext()\n\n  const { label }: { label: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<form.Subscribe selector={(state) => state.isSubmitting}>\n  {#snippet children(isSubmitting)}\n    <button type=\"submit\" disabled={isSubmitting}>\n      {label}\n    </button>\n  {/snippet}\n</form.Subscribe>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"code","value":"// form.ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\nimport TextField from './text-field.svelte'\nimport SubscribeButton from './subscribe-button.svelte'\n\nexport const { createAppForm, getFormType } = createFormCreator({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- App.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<form.AppForm>\n  <!-- Notice the `AppForm` component wrapper; `AppForm` provides the required context -->\n  {#snippet children()}\n    <form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n  {/snippet}\n</form.AppForm>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"breaking-big-forms-into-smaller-pieces","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Breaking big forms into smaller pieces"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sometimes forms get very large; it's just how it goes sometimes. While TanStack Form supports large forms well, it's never fun to work with hundreds or thousands of lines of code long files."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To solve this, you can break forms into smaller Svelte components that accept the form as a prop."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// shared-form.ts\nimport { formOptions } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\nexport const peopleFormOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: 'John',\n    lastName: 'Doe',\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- child-form.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { getFormType } from './form.js'\n  import { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\n\n  const formType = getFormType({ ...peopleFormOpts })\n\n  const { form, title = 'Child Form' }: { form: typeof formType; title?: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<div>\n  <p>{title}</p>\n  <form.AppField name=\"firstName\">\n    {#snippet children(field)}\n      <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppField>\n  <form.AppForm>\n    {#snippet children()}\n      <form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppForm>\n</div>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- App.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n  import { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\n  import ChildForm from './child-form.svelte'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    ...peopleFormOpts,\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<ChildForm {form} title=\"Testing\" />","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"getting-the-form-type","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Getting the Form Type"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you split a form into smaller child components, those child components need a typed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form"},{"type":"text","value":" prop so that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.AppField"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.AppForm"},{"type":"text","value":", and the registered field/form components remain fully type-safe. Writing out the full "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createAppForm"},{"type":"text","value":" generics by hand is verbose and error-prone."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFormCreator"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getFormType"},{"type":"text","value":" helper alongside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createAppForm"},{"type":"text","value":" for exactly this purpose. It accepts the same options object as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createAppForm"},{"type":"text","value":" and returns a value whose type matches the form instance that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createAppForm"},{"type":"text","value":" would produce — without actually running any logic at runtime."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// form.ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\nimport TextField from './text-field.svelte'\nimport SubscribeButton from './subscribe-button.svelte'\n\nexport const { createAppForm, getFormType } = createFormCreator({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use it together with your shared "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"formOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" to derive the expected "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form"},{"type":"text","value":" prop type:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- child-form.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { getFormType } from './form.js'\n  import { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\n\n  const formType = getFormType({ ...peopleFormOpts })\n\n  const { form }: { form: typeof formType } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<form.AppField name=\"firstName\">\n  {#snippet children(field)}\n    <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />\n  {/snippet}\n</form.AppField>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This gives the child component a fully typed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form"},{"type":"text","value":" prop — including the registered "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fieldComponents"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"formComponents"},{"type":"text","value":" — without having to repeat the form generics by hand or maintain a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReturnType<typeof createAppForm<...>>"},{"type":"text","value":" alias."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useFormContext"},{"type":"text","value":" is still the best fit for registered form components rendered inside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.AppForm"},{"type":"text","value":". Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getFormType"},{"type":"text","value":" when you pass the form instance down as a prop to a child component."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reusing-groups-of-fields-in-multiple-forms","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reusing groups of fields in multiple forms"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sometimes, a pair of fields are so closely related that it makes sense to group and reuse them — like the password example listed in the "},{"type":"link","href":"./linked-fields.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"linked fields guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Instead of repeating this logic across multiple forms, you can create reusable field group components."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unlike form-level components, validators in field groups cannot be strictly typed and could be any value.\nEnsure that your fields can accept unknown error types."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rewriting the passwords example as a reusable component would look like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- password-fields.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import type { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n\n  type PasswordFields = {\n    password: string\n    confirm_password: string\n  }\n\n  type AppForm = ReturnType<typeof createAppForm<PasswordFields>>\n\n  const { form, title = 'Password' }: { form: AppForm; title?: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<div>\n  <h2>{title}</h2>\n  <form.AppField name=\"password\">\n    {#snippet children(field)}\n      <field.TextField label=\"Password\" />\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppField>\n  <form.AppField\n    name=\"confirm_password\"\n    validators={{\n      onChangeListenTo: ['password'],\n      onChange: ({ value, fieldApi }) => {\n        if (value !== fieldApi.form.getFieldValue('password')) {\n          return 'Passwords do not match'\n        }\n        return undefined\n      },\n    }}\n  >\n    {#snippet children(field)}\n      <div>\n        <field.TextField label=\"Confirm Password\" />\n        {#each field.state.meta.errors as error}\n          <div style=\"color: red;\">{error}</div>\n        {/each}\n      </div>\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppField>\n</div>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"We can now use these grouped fields in any form that implements the required fields:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- App.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n  import PasswordFields from './password-fields.svelte'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      name: '',\n      age: 0,\n      password: '',\n      confirm_password: '',\n    },\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<form.AppForm>\n  {#snippet children()}\n    <form.AppField name=\"name\">\n      {#snippet children(field)}\n        <field.TextField label=\"Name\" />\n      {/snippet}\n    </form.AppField>\n    <PasswordFields {form} title=\"Account Password\" />\n    <form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n  {/snippet}\n</form.AppForm>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tree-shaking-form-and-field-components","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tree-shaking form and field components"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While the above examples are great for getting started, they're not ideal for certain use-cases where you might have hundreds of form and field components.\nIn particular, you may not want to include all of your form and field components in the bundle of every file that uses your form method."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To solve this, you can use dynamic imports with Svelte's component loading:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/utils/form-context.ts\nimport { createFormCreatorContexts } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\nexport const { useFieldContext, useFormContext } = createFormCreatorContexts()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- src/components/text-field.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { useFieldContext } from '../utils/form-context.js'\n\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n\n  const { label }: { label: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<label>\n  <div>{label}</div>\n  <input\n    value={field.state.value}\n    oninput={(e) => field.handleChange((e.target as HTMLInputElement).value)}\n  />\n</label>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"code","value":"// src/utils/form.ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\nimport TextField from '../components/text-field.svelte'\nimport SubscribeButton from '../components/subscribe-button.svelte'\n\nexport const { createAppForm, getFormType } = createFormCreator({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- src/App.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import PeoplePage from './features/people/page.svelte'\n</script>\n\n<PeoplePage />","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"putting-it-all-together","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Putting it all together"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now that we've covered the basics of creating custom form methods, let's put it all together in a single example."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// /src/utils/form-context.ts, to be used across the entire app\nimport { createFormCreatorContexts } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\nexport const { useFieldContext, useFormContext } = createFormCreatorContexts()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- /src/components/text-field.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { useFieldContext } from '../utils/form-context.js'\n\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n\n  const { label }: { label: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<label>\n  <div>{label}</div>\n  <input\n    value={field.state.value}\n    oninput={(e) => field.handleChange((e.target as HTMLInputElement).value)}\n  />\n</label>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- /src/components/subscribe-button.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { useFormContext } from '../utils/form-context.js'\n\n  const form = useFormContext()\n\n  const { label }: { label: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<form.Subscribe selector={(state) => state.isSubmitting}>\n  {#snippet children(isSubmitting)}\n    <button type=\"submit\" disabled={isSubmitting}>\n      {label}\n    </button>\n  {/snippet}\n</form.Subscribe>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"code","value":"// /src/utils/form.ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\nimport TextField from '../components/text-field.svelte'\nimport SubscribeButton from '../components/subscribe-button.svelte'\n\nexport const { createAppForm, getFormType } = createFormCreator({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"// /src/features/people/shared-form.ts, to be used across `people` features\nimport { formOptions } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\nexport const peopleFormOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: 'John',\n    lastName: 'Doe',\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- /src/features/people/child-form.svelte, to be used in the `people` page -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { getFormType } from '../../utils/form.js'\n  import { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\n\n  const formType = getFormType({ ...peopleFormOpts })\n\n  const { form, title = 'Child Form' }: { form: typeof formType; title?: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<div>\n  <p>{title}</p>\n  <form.AppField name=\"firstName\">\n    {#snippet children(field)}\n      <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppField>\n  <form.AppForm>\n    {#snippet children()}\n      <form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppForm>\n</div>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- /src/features/people/page.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from '../../utils/form.js'\n  import { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\n  import ChildForm from './child-form.svelte'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    ...peopleFormOpts,\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<ChildForm {form} title=\"Testing\" />","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"api-usage-guidance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"API Usage Guidance"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's a chart to help you decide what APIs you should be using:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"image","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/form/main/docs/assets/svelte_form_composability.svg","alt":""}]}],"headings":[{"id":"custom-form-methods","text":"Custom Form Methods","level":2},{"id":"pre-bound-field-components","text":"Pre-bound Field Components","level":3},{"id":"pre-bound-form-components","text":"Pre-bound Form Components","level":3},{"id":"breaking-big-forms-into-smaller-pieces","text":"Breaking big forms into smaller pieces","level":2},{"id":"getting-the-form-type","text":"Getting the Form Type","level":2},{"id":"reusing-groups-of-fields-in-multiple-forms","text":"Reusing groups of fields in multiple forms","level":2},{"id":"tree-shaking-form-and-field-components","text":"Tree-shaking form and field components","level":2},{"id":"putting-it-all-together","text":"Putting it all together","level":2},{"id":"api-usage-guidance","text":"API Usage Guidance","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nA common criticism of TanStack Form is its verbosity out-of-the-box. While this _can_ be useful for educational purposes - helping enforce understanding our APIs - it's not ideal in production use cases.\n\nAs a result, while `form.Field` enables the most powerful and flexible usage of TanStack Form, we provide APIs that wrap it and make your application code less verbose.\n\n## Custom Form Methods\n\nThe most powerful way to compose forms is to create custom form methods. This allows you to create a form method that is tailored to your application's needs, including pre-bound custom UI components and more.\n\nAt its most basic, `createFormCreator` is a function that returns a `createAppForm` method.\n\n> This un-customized `createAppForm` method is identical to `createForm`, but that will quickly change as we add more options to `createFormCreator`.\n\n```ts\n// form-context.ts\nimport { createFormCreatorContexts } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\n// export useFieldContext and useFormContext for use in your custom components\nexport const { useFieldContext, useFormContext } = createFormCreatorContexts()\n```\n\n```ts\n// form.ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\nexport const { createAppForm } = createFormCreator({\n  // We'll learn more about these options later\n  fieldComponents: {},\n  formComponents: {},\n})\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- App.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    // Supports all createForm options\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<form.Field name=\"firstName\">\n  <!-- ... -->\n</form.Field>\n```\n\n### Pre-bound Field Components\n\nOnce this scaffolding is in place, you can start adding custom field and form components to your form method.\n\n> Note: the `useFieldContext` must be the same one exported from your custom form context\n\n```svelte\n<!-- text-field.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { useFieldContext } from './form-context.js'\n\n  // The `Field` infers that it should have a `value` type of `string`\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n\n  const { label }: { label: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<label>\n  <div>{label}</div>\n  <input\n    value={field.state.value}\n    oninput={(e) => field.handleChange((e.target as HTMLInputElement).value)}\n  />\n</label>\n```\n\nYou're then able to register this component with your form method.\n\n```ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\nimport TextField from './text-field.svelte'\n\nexport const { createAppForm } = createFormCreator({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {},\n})\n```\n\nAnd use it in your form:\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<!-- Notice the `AppField` instead of `Field`; `AppField` provides the required context -->\n<form.AppField name=\"firstName\">\n  {#snippet children(field)}\n    <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />\n  {/snippet}\n</form.AppField>\n```\n\nThis not only allows you to reuse the UI of your shared component, but retains the type-safety you'd expect from TanStack Form: Typo `name` and get a TypeScript error.\n\n### Pre-bound Form Components\n\nWhile `form.AppField` solves many of the problems with Field boilerplate and reusability, it doesn't solve the problem of _form_ boilerplate and reusability.\n\nIn particular, being able to share instances of `form.Subscribe` for, say, a reactive form submission button is a common usecase.\n\n```svelte\n<!-- subscribe-button.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { useFormContext } from './form-context.js'\n\n  const form = useFormContext()\n\n  const { label }: { label: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<form.Subscribe selector={(state) => state.isSubmitting}>\n  {#snippet children(isSubmitting)}\n    <button type=\"submit\" disabled={isSubmitting}>\n      {label}\n    </button>\n  {/snippet}\n</form.Subscribe>\n```\n\n```ts\n// form.ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\nimport TextField from './text-field.svelte'\nimport SubscribeButton from './subscribe-button.svelte'\n\nexport const { createAppForm, getFormType } = createFormCreator({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- App.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<form.AppForm>\n  <!-- Notice the `AppForm` component wrapper; `AppForm` provides the required context -->\n  {#snippet children()}\n    <form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n  {/snippet}\n</form.AppForm>\n```\n\n## Breaking big forms into smaller pieces\n\nSometimes forms get very large; it's just how it goes sometimes. While TanStack Form supports large forms well, it's never fun to work with hundreds or thousands of lines of code long files.\n\nTo solve this, you can break forms into smaller Svelte components that accept the form as a prop.\n\n```ts\n// shared-form.ts\nimport { formOptions } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\nexport const peopleFormOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: 'John',\n    lastName: 'Doe',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- child-form.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { getFormType } from './form.js'\n  import { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\n\n  const formType = getFormType({ ...peopleFormOpts })\n\n  const { form, title = 'Child Form' }: { form: typeof formType; title?: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<div>\n  <p>{title}</p>\n  <form.AppField name=\"firstName\">\n    {#snippet children(field)}\n      <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppField>\n  <form.AppForm>\n    {#snippet children()}\n      <form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppForm>\n</div>\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- App.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n  import { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\n  import ChildForm from './child-form.svelte'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    ...peopleFormOpts,\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<ChildForm {form} title=\"Testing\" />\n```\n\n## Getting the Form Type\n\nWhen you split a form into smaller child components, those child components need a typed `form` prop so that `form.AppField`, `form.AppForm`, and the registered field/form components remain fully type-safe. Writing out the full `createAppForm` generics by hand is verbose and error-prone.\n\n`createFormCreator` returns a `getFormType` helper alongside `createAppForm` for exactly this purpose. It accepts the same options object as `createAppForm` and returns a value whose type matches the form instance that `createAppForm` would produce — without actually running any logic at runtime.\n\n```ts\n// form.ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\nimport TextField from './text-field.svelte'\nimport SubscribeButton from './subscribe-button.svelte'\n\nexport const { createAppForm, getFormType } = createFormCreator({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nUse it together with your shared `formOptions` to derive the expected `form` prop type:\n\n```svelte\n<!-- child-form.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { getFormType } from './form.js'\n  import { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\n\n  const formType = getFormType({ ...peopleFormOpts })\n\n  const { form }: { form: typeof formType } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<form.AppField name=\"firstName\">\n  {#snippet children(field)}\n    <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />\n  {/snippet}\n</form.AppField>\n```\n\nThis gives the child component a fully typed `form` prop — including the registered `fieldComponents` and `formComponents` — without having to repeat the form generics by hand or maintain a `ReturnType<typeof createAppForm<...>>` alias.\n\n> `useFormContext` is still the best fit for registered form components rendered inside `form.AppForm`. Use `getFormType` when you pass the form instance down as a prop to a child component.\n\n## Reusing groups of fields in multiple forms\n\nSometimes, a pair of fields are so closely related that it makes sense to group and reuse them — like the password example listed in the [linked fields guide](./linked-fields.md). Instead of repeating this logic across multiple forms, you can create reusable field group components.\n\n> Unlike form-level components, validators in field groups cannot be strictly typed and could be any value.\n> Ensure that your fields can accept unknown error types.\n\nRewriting the passwords example as a reusable component would look like this:\n\n```svelte\n<!-- password-fields.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import type { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n\n  type PasswordFields = {\n    password: string\n    confirm_password: string\n  }\n\n  type AppForm = ReturnType<typeof createAppForm<PasswordFields>>\n\n  const { form, title = 'Password' }: { form: AppForm; title?: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<div>\n  <h2>{title}</h2>\n  <form.AppField name=\"password\">\n    {#snippet children(field)}\n      <field.TextField label=\"Password\" />\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppField>\n  <form.AppField\n    name=\"confirm_password\"\n    validators={{\n      onChangeListenTo: ['password'],\n      onChange: ({ value, fieldApi }) => {\n        if (value !== fieldApi.form.getFieldValue('password')) {\n          return 'Passwords do not match'\n        }\n        return undefined\n      },\n    }}\n  >\n    {#snippet children(field)}\n      <div>\n        <field.TextField label=\"Confirm Password\" />\n        {#each field.state.meta.errors as error}\n          <div style=\"color: red;\">{error}</div>\n        {/each}\n      </div>\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppField>\n</div>\n```\n\nWe can now use these grouped fields in any form that implements the required fields:\n\n```svelte\n<!-- App.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n  import PasswordFields from './password-fields.svelte'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      name: '',\n      age: 0,\n      password: '',\n      confirm_password: '',\n    },\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<form.AppForm>\n  {#snippet children()}\n    <form.AppField name=\"name\">\n      {#snippet children(field)}\n        <field.TextField label=\"Name\" />\n      {/snippet}\n    </form.AppField>\n    <PasswordFields {form} title=\"Account Password\" />\n    <form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n  {/snippet}\n</form.AppForm>\n```\n\n## Tree-shaking form and field components\n\nWhile the above examples are great for getting started, they're not ideal for certain use-cases where you might have hundreds of form and field components.\nIn particular, you may not want to include all of your form and field components in the bundle of every file that uses your form method.\n\nTo solve this, you can use dynamic imports with Svelte's component loading:\n\n```ts\n// src/utils/form-context.ts\nimport { createFormCreatorContexts } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\nexport const { useFieldContext, useFormContext } = createFormCreatorContexts()\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- src/components/text-field.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { useFieldContext } from '../utils/form-context.js'\n\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n\n  const { label }: { label: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<label>\n  <div>{label}</div>\n  <input\n    value={field.state.value}\n    oninput={(e) => field.handleChange((e.target as HTMLInputElement).value)}\n  />\n</label>\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/utils/form.ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\nimport TextField from '../components/text-field.svelte'\nimport SubscribeButton from '../components/subscribe-button.svelte'\n\nexport const { createAppForm, getFormType } = createFormCreator({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- src/App.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import PeoplePage from './features/people/page.svelte'\n</script>\n\n<PeoplePage />\n```\n\n## Putting it all together\n\nNow that we've covered the basics of creating custom form methods, let's put it all together in a single example.\n\n```ts\n// /src/utils/form-context.ts, to be used across the entire app\nimport { createFormCreatorContexts } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\nexport const { useFieldContext, useFormContext } = createFormCreatorContexts()\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- /src/components/text-field.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { useFieldContext } from '../utils/form-context.js'\n\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n\n  const { label }: { label: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<label>\n  <div>{label}</div>\n  <input\n    value={field.state.value}\n    oninput={(e) => field.handleChange((e.target as HTMLInputElement).value)}\n  />\n</label>\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- /src/components/subscribe-button.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { useFormContext } from '../utils/form-context.js'\n\n  const form = useFormContext()\n\n  const { label }: { label: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<form.Subscribe selector={(state) => state.isSubmitting}>\n  {#snippet children(isSubmitting)}\n    <button type=\"submit\" disabled={isSubmitting}>\n      {label}\n    </button>\n  {/snippet}\n</form.Subscribe>\n```\n\n```ts\n// /src/utils/form.ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\nimport TextField from '../components/text-field.svelte'\nimport SubscribeButton from '../components/subscribe-button.svelte'\n\nexport const { createAppForm, getFormType } = createFormCreator({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts\n// /src/features/people/shared-form.ts, to be used across `people` features\nimport { formOptions } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\nexport const peopleFormOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: 'John',\n    lastName: 'Doe',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- /src/features/people/child-form.svelte, to be used in the `people` page -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { getFormType } from '../../utils/form.js'\n  import { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\n\n  const formType = getFormType({ ...peopleFormOpts })\n\n  const { form, title = 'Child Form' }: { form: typeof formType; title?: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<div>\n  <p>{title}</p>\n  <form.AppField name=\"firstName\">\n    {#snippet children(field)}\n      <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppField>\n  <form.AppForm>\n    {#snippet children()}\n      <form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppForm>\n</div>\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- /src/features/people/page.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from '../../utils/form.js'\n  import { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\n  import ChildForm from './child-form.svelte'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    ...peopleFormOpts,\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<ChildForm {form} title=\"Testing\" />\n```\n\n## API Usage Guidance\n\nHere's a chart to help you decide what APIs you should be using:\n\n![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/form/main/docs/assets/svelte_form_composability.svg)\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: form-composition\ntitle: Form Composition\n---\n\nA common criticism of TanStack Form is its verbosity out-of-the-box. While this _can_ be useful for educational purposes - helping enforce understanding our APIs - it's not ideal in production use cases.\n\nAs a result, while `form.Field` enables the most powerful and flexible usage of TanStack Form, we provide APIs that wrap it and make your application code less verbose.\n\n## Custom Form Methods\n\nThe most powerful way to compose forms is to create custom form methods. This allows you to create a form method that is tailored to your application's needs, including pre-bound custom UI components and more.\n\nAt its most basic, `createFormCreator` is a function that returns a `createAppForm` method.\n\n> This un-customized `createAppForm` method is identical to `createForm`, but that will quickly change as we add more options to `createFormCreator`.\n\n```ts\n// form-context.ts\nimport { createFormCreatorContexts } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\n// export useFieldContext and useFormContext for use in your custom components\nexport const { useFieldContext, useFormContext } = createFormCreatorContexts()\n```\n\n```ts\n// form.ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\nexport const { createAppForm } = createFormCreator({\n  // We'll learn more about these options later\n  fieldComponents: {},\n  formComponents: {},\n})\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- App.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    // Supports all createForm options\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<form.Field name=\"firstName\">\n  <!-- ... -->\n</form.Field>\n```\n\n### Pre-bound Field Components\n\nOnce this scaffolding is in place, you can start adding custom field and form components to your form method.\n\n> Note: the `useFieldContext` must be the same one exported from your custom form context\n\n```svelte\n<!-- text-field.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { useFieldContext } from './form-context.js'\n\n  // The `Field` infers that it should have a `value` type of `string`\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n\n  const { label }: { label: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<label>\n  <div>{label}</div>\n  <input\n    value={field.state.value}\n    oninput={(e) => field.handleChange((e.target as HTMLInputElement).value)}\n  />\n</label>\n```\n\nYou're then able to register this component with your form method.\n\n```ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\nimport TextField from './text-field.svelte'\n\nexport const { createAppForm } = createFormCreator({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {},\n})\n```\n\nAnd use it in your form:\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<!-- Notice the `AppField` instead of `Field`; `AppField` provides the required context -->\n<form.AppField name=\"firstName\">\n  {#snippet children(field)}\n    <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />\n  {/snippet}\n</form.AppField>\n```\n\nThis not only allows you to reuse the UI of your shared component, but retains the type-safety you'd expect from TanStack Form: Typo `name` and get a TypeScript error.\n\n### Pre-bound Form Components\n\nWhile `form.AppField` solves many of the problems with Field boilerplate and reusability, it doesn't solve the problem of _form_ boilerplate and reusability.\n\nIn particular, being able to share instances of `form.Subscribe` for, say, a reactive form submission button is a common usecase.\n\n```svelte\n<!-- subscribe-button.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { useFormContext } from './form-context.js'\n\n  const form = useFormContext()\n\n  const { label }: { label: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<form.Subscribe selector={(state) => state.isSubmitting}>\n  {#snippet children(isSubmitting)}\n    <button type=\"submit\" disabled={isSubmitting}>\n      {label}\n    </button>\n  {/snippet}\n</form.Subscribe>\n```\n\n```ts\n// form.ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\nimport TextField from './text-field.svelte'\nimport SubscribeButton from './subscribe-button.svelte'\n\nexport const { createAppForm, getFormType } = createFormCreator({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- App.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<form.AppForm>\n  <!-- Notice the `AppForm` component wrapper; `AppForm` provides the required context -->\n  {#snippet children()}\n    <form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n  {/snippet}\n</form.AppForm>\n```\n\n## Breaking big forms into smaller pieces\n\nSometimes forms get very large; it's just how it goes sometimes. While TanStack Form supports large forms well, it's never fun to work with hundreds or thousands of lines of code long files.\n\nTo solve this, you can break forms into smaller Svelte components that accept the form as a prop.\n\n```ts\n// shared-form.ts\nimport { formOptions } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\nexport const peopleFormOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: 'John',\n    lastName: 'Doe',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- child-form.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { getFormType } from './form.js'\n  import { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\n\n  const formType = getFormType({ ...peopleFormOpts })\n\n  const { form, title = 'Child Form' }: { form: typeof formType; title?: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<div>\n  <p>{title}</p>\n  <form.AppField name=\"firstName\">\n    {#snippet children(field)}\n      <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppField>\n  <form.AppForm>\n    {#snippet children()}\n      <form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppForm>\n</div>\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- App.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n  import { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\n  import ChildForm from './child-form.svelte'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    ...peopleFormOpts,\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<ChildForm {form} title=\"Testing\" />\n```\n\n## Getting the Form Type\n\nWhen you split a form into smaller child components, those child components need a typed `form` prop so that `form.AppField`, `form.AppForm`, and the registered field/form components remain fully type-safe. Writing out the full `createAppForm` generics by hand is verbose and error-prone.\n\n`createFormCreator` returns a `getFormType` helper alongside `createAppForm` for exactly this purpose. It accepts the same options object as `createAppForm` and returns a value whose type matches the form instance that `createAppForm` would produce — without actually running any logic at runtime.\n\n```ts\n// form.ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\nimport TextField from './text-field.svelte'\nimport SubscribeButton from './subscribe-button.svelte'\n\nexport const { createAppForm, getFormType } = createFormCreator({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nUse it together with your shared `formOptions` to derive the expected `form` prop type:\n\n```svelte\n<!-- child-form.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { getFormType } from './form.js'\n  import { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\n\n  const formType = getFormType({ ...peopleFormOpts })\n\n  const { form }: { form: typeof formType } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<form.AppField name=\"firstName\">\n  {#snippet children(field)}\n    <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />\n  {/snippet}\n</form.AppField>\n```\n\nThis gives the child component a fully typed `form` prop — including the registered `fieldComponents` and `formComponents` — without having to repeat the form generics by hand or maintain a `ReturnType<typeof createAppForm<...>>` alias.\n\n> `useFormContext` is still the best fit for registered form components rendered inside `form.AppForm`. Use `getFormType` when you pass the form instance down as a prop to a child component.\n\n## Reusing groups of fields in multiple forms\n\nSometimes, a pair of fields are so closely related that it makes sense to group and reuse them — like the password example listed in the [linked fields guide](./linked-fields.md). Instead of repeating this logic across multiple forms, you can create reusable field group components.\n\n> Unlike form-level components, validators in field groups cannot be strictly typed and could be any value.\n> Ensure that your fields can accept unknown error types.\n\nRewriting the passwords example as a reusable component would look like this:\n\n```svelte\n<!-- password-fields.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import type { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n\n  type PasswordFields = {\n    password: string\n    confirm_password: string\n  }\n\n  type AppForm = ReturnType<typeof createAppForm<PasswordFields>>\n\n  const { form, title = 'Password' }: { form: AppForm; title?: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<div>\n  <h2>{title}</h2>\n  <form.AppField name=\"password\">\n    {#snippet children(field)}\n      <field.TextField label=\"Password\" />\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppField>\n  <form.AppField\n    name=\"confirm_password\"\n    validators={{\n      onChangeListenTo: ['password'],\n      onChange: ({ value, fieldApi }) => {\n        if (value !== fieldApi.form.getFieldValue('password')) {\n          return 'Passwords do not match'\n        }\n        return undefined\n      },\n    }}\n  >\n    {#snippet children(field)}\n      <div>\n        <field.TextField label=\"Confirm Password\" />\n        {#each field.state.meta.errors as error}\n          <div style=\"color: red;\">{error}</div>\n        {/each}\n      </div>\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppField>\n</div>\n```\n\nWe can now use these grouped fields in any form that implements the required fields:\n\n```svelte\n<!-- App.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from './form.js'\n  import PasswordFields from './password-fields.svelte'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      name: '',\n      age: 0,\n      password: '',\n      confirm_password: '',\n    },\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<form.AppForm>\n  {#snippet children()}\n    <form.AppField name=\"name\">\n      {#snippet children(field)}\n        <field.TextField label=\"Name\" />\n      {/snippet}\n    </form.AppField>\n    <PasswordFields {form} title=\"Account Password\" />\n    <form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n  {/snippet}\n</form.AppForm>\n```\n\n## Tree-shaking form and field components\n\nWhile the above examples are great for getting started, they're not ideal for certain use-cases where you might have hundreds of form and field components.\nIn particular, you may not want to include all of your form and field components in the bundle of every file that uses your form method.\n\nTo solve this, you can use dynamic imports with Svelte's component loading:\n\n```ts\n// src/utils/form-context.ts\nimport { createFormCreatorContexts } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\nexport const { useFieldContext, useFormContext } = createFormCreatorContexts()\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- src/components/text-field.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { useFieldContext } from '../utils/form-context.js'\n\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n\n  const { label }: { label: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<label>\n  <div>{label}</div>\n  <input\n    value={field.state.value}\n    oninput={(e) => field.handleChange((e.target as HTMLInputElement).value)}\n  />\n</label>\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/utils/form.ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\nimport TextField from '../components/text-field.svelte'\nimport SubscribeButton from '../components/subscribe-button.svelte'\n\nexport const { createAppForm, getFormType } = createFormCreator({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- src/App.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import PeoplePage from './features/people/page.svelte'\n</script>\n\n<PeoplePage />\n```\n\n## Putting it all together\n\nNow that we've covered the basics of creating custom form methods, let's put it all together in a single example.\n\n```ts\n// /src/utils/form-context.ts, to be used across the entire app\nimport { createFormCreatorContexts } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\nexport const { useFieldContext, useFormContext } = createFormCreatorContexts()\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- /src/components/text-field.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { useFieldContext } from '../utils/form-context.js'\n\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n\n  const { label }: { label: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<label>\n  <div>{label}</div>\n  <input\n    value={field.state.value}\n    oninput={(e) => field.handleChange((e.target as HTMLInputElement).value)}\n  />\n</label>\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- /src/components/subscribe-button.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { useFormContext } from '../utils/form-context.js'\n\n  const form = useFormContext()\n\n  const { label }: { label: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<form.Subscribe selector={(state) => state.isSubmitting}>\n  {#snippet children(isSubmitting)}\n    <button type=\"submit\" disabled={isSubmitting}>\n      {label}\n    </button>\n  {/snippet}\n</form.Subscribe>\n```\n\n```ts\n// /src/utils/form.ts\nimport { createFormCreator } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\nimport TextField from '../components/text-field.svelte'\nimport SubscribeButton from '../components/subscribe-button.svelte'\n\nexport const { createAppForm, getFormType } = createFormCreator({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts\n// /src/features/people/shared-form.ts, to be used across `people` features\nimport { formOptions } from '@tanstack/svelte-form'\n\nexport const peopleFormOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: 'John',\n    lastName: 'Doe',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- /src/features/people/child-form.svelte, to be used in the `people` page -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { getFormType } from '../../utils/form.js'\n  import { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\n\n  const formType = getFormType({ ...peopleFormOpts })\n\n  const { form, title = 'Child Form' }: { form: typeof formType; title?: string } = $props()\n</script>\n\n<div>\n  <p>{title}</p>\n  <form.AppField name=\"firstName\">\n    {#snippet children(field)}\n      <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppField>\n  <form.AppForm>\n    {#snippet children()}\n      <form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n    {/snippet}\n  </form.AppForm>\n</div>\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- /src/features/people/page.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createAppForm } from '../../utils/form.js'\n  import { peopleFormOpts } from './shared-form.js'\n  import ChildForm from './child-form.svelte'\n\n  const form = createAppForm(() => ({\n    ...peopleFormOpts,\n  }))\n</script>\n\n<ChildForm {form} title=\"Testing\" />\n```\n\n## API Usage Guidance\n\nHere's a chart to help you decide what APIs you should be using:\n\n![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/form/main/docs/assets/svelte_form_composability.svg)\n"}
